    "question": "Short Answer: Describe each of the four quadrants of the BCG matrix — stars, question marks, cash cows, and poor dogs — in terms of their market share and growth rate characteristics, and the recommended strategy for each.",
    "answer": "Stars: High market share, high growth rate — established products in an attractive market. Require high investment in marketing, but provide high earnings. Strategy: invest to strengthen and secure their competitive advantage. When growth flattens, stars become cash cows.\n\nQuestion Marks: Low market share, high growth rate — mostly new products needing large financial commitment. High risk of failure. Strategy: management must decide which question marks to invest in and which to take off the market. Financial investment comes from cash cows.\n\nCash Cows: High market share, low growth rate — established products in a market with declining attractiveness. Low financial investment required, good revenue. Strategy: use cash flow surplus to finance stars and question marks.\n\nPoor Dogs: Low market share, low growth rate — unattractive products in unattractive markets with little future potential. Revenue contribution is negative or balanced. Strategy: do not invest; free up resources and redirect financial surpluses to stars and question marks."
